#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "The semantic layer"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "The semantic layer is where every measure and indicator is defined, once, in version control. Dashboards, natural-language query, agents, published statistics and international returns all read from it, so an enrolment ratio means the same thing on every screen, in every file and for every release ([P11]{1}, [CAP-SEMANTICS]{2})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "It matters more for an EMIS than for most platforms. Official indicators are compared across years, areas and countries; a definition that drifts between two tools produces two \"official\" figures, and only one of them can be right."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "How it works"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "Why it is drawn this way"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "**Definitions sit on top of gold counts.** Gold stores additive counts; every ratio is computed by the semantic layer from them, so any level of aggregation gives the right answer ([CAP-TRANSFORMATION.5]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "**Everything a consumer reads is generated.** Engine-native metric objects and saved-query tables are produced from the definitions by CI, never written by hand ([CAP-SEMANTICS.8]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "**A parity test closes the loop.** Every serving path must return the same numbers for every saved query, which is what \"defined once\" means in practice ([CAP-SEMANTICS.9]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "**Published statistics come from saved-query tables,** stamped with the definition version and reference date, never from a live query."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "Rules that keep one definition portable"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "**Model indicators as ratios of additive counts on one gold table.** An indicator's numerator and denominator are counts at the same grain (reference date, area, sex, age, level and so on). An external denominator, such as a population estimate, is joined in gold with its version, so every engine computes the ratio the same way."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "**Use a portable subset of metric types:** simple counts and sums, ratios, and derived expressions without time offsets. Anything else (cumulative windows, offsets, conversion funnels) needs a recorded, tested mapping for each serving engine ([CAP-SEMANTICS.7]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "**Keep indicator metadata with the definition** and check it in CI: code, the edition of the international definition followed, unit, reference-date rule, allowed disaggregations, coverage rule, owner and version ([CAP-SEMANTICS.3]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "**Version the definitions with the models** that produce their counts, in the same project and the same review ([CAP-SEMANTICS.6]{1})."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "In the reference implementations"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/architecture/semantic-layer.md
msgid "Both reference implementations use the **dbt semantic layer** ([ADR-0011]{1}): semantic models and metrics written in dbt's YAML specification (the latest spec, dbt 1.12 and later), validated and compiled by **MetricFlow**, which is Apache-2.0 licensed."
msgstr ""

